Major models / “sets” of intelligence
1) Gardner’s Multiple Intelligences

(very popular for thinking beyond “school smarts”)

Linguistic — words, storytelling, writing (poet, lawyer).

Logical-mathematical — math, logic, puzzles (scientist).

Spatial — visualizing space, maps, design (architect).

Musical — rhythm, melody, composition (musician).

Bodily-kinesthetic — fine motor, sports, crafts (dancer, craftsperson).

Interpersonal — reading others, teamwork (therapist, leader).

Intrapersonal — self-awareness, introspection (philosopher).

Naturalistic — noticing nature patterns (ecologist).

Existential (proposed) — big-picture meaning questions.

2) Sternberg’s Triarchic Theory

Analytical (problem solving, tests)

Creative (novel thinking, innovation)

Practical (“street smarts”, applying ideas in real life)

3) Fluid vs Crystallized (Cattell)

Fluid intelligence = reasoning, solving new problems (peaks younger).

Crystallized intelligence = knowledge & skills from experience (grows with learning).

4) Emotional Intelligence (EQ)

Recognizing/handling your emotions and others’ — important for leadership, relationships.

5) Social / Practical / Adaptive Intelligence

Reading groups, negotiating, surviving & thriving in messy real-world settings.

6) Creative / Divergent Thinking

Generating many novel ideas, making unusual connections.

7) General intelligence (g) & IQ

A statistical factor that shows up across tests. IQ tests try to measure it — useful but limited and culturally biased.

8) Domain-specific & Expertise-based intelligence

Deep skills in a field (e.g., chess, coding, carpentry). Practice + feedback produce this.

9) Collective / Group Intelligence

How smart a team/community is together (coordination, shared knowledge).

10) Embodied & Sensorimotor intelligence

Intelligence in movement, perception and interacting physically with the world (important for makers, athletes).

What “genius” means (types & markers)

Genius isn’t a single scientific category — it’s usually used when someone shows exceptional ability that’s original, deep, productive, and/or has big impact. Common ways people talk about genius:

Intellectual/Analytical genius — extreme problem-solving (e.g., deep math/physics breakthroughs).

Creative/artistic genius — originality + emotional depth that changes an art form.

Technical/engineering genius — inventing practical, transformative tech.

Practical/strategic genius — leaders who navigate complex systems brilliantly.

Prodigy vs Late Bloomer — some show genius early; others produce greatest work later.

Savant & neurodivergent genius — extreme ability in a narrow domain (memory, music) sometimes alongside other differences.

Typical markers people use: consistently novel ideas, high productivity/skill, deep domain knowledge, and influence on others/field. IQ scores sometimes used (e.g., 140+), but IQ is only one piece and doesn’t guarantee creative impact.

How to spot & develop strengths (practical)

Observe patterns — what do you learn fast? what feels effortless? (that’s often a strength).

Deliberate practice — expertise comes from focused practice + feedback.

Mix types — combine a strength (e.g., craft skill) with creativity to get unique results.

Environment — supportive peers, time to tinker, low judgement helps creative genius.

For neurodivergent folks (ADHD, dyslexia, etc.): your brain may favour big-picture, visual, hands-on or creative thinking — lean into those and use tools (checklists, dictation, timers) to manage administrative friction. Your differences can be powerful strengths in pattern recognition, creative problem framing, and rapid idea generation.

Quick cheat-summary

Think broad: there’s verbal, logical, spatial, musical, bodily, social, self, naturalistic, practical, creative, emotional.

“Genius” = exceptional impact + originality — comes in many flavors (not just IQ).

Develop it: focused practice, the right environment, feedback, and using your natural cognitive style.

